Cleanroom programming and energy — Tschudi, Sartor and Xu (@cm-lbnl-source: 0, is #reference, #part-of @cm-sources) { r_36R5RS6HEWBTYN4SHCVPQNP12X
Bill Tschudi, Dale Sartor and Tengfang Xu. *An Energy Efficiency Guide for Use in Cleanroom Programming*, LBNL-49223, December 2001. [Institutional record](https://eta-publications.lbl.gov/publications/cleanroom-energy-programming-guide) · [Complete 42-page PDF](https://eta-publications.lbl.gov/sites/default/files/lbnl-49223.pdf).

Read the whole guide. Translate a proposed product and process into architectural, mechanical and electrical requirements before selecting equipment. Revisit its energy reasoning when air recirculation, filtration, heat loads and operating schedules become quantitative.

Cover, legal notice, contents, institutional metadata and selected contamination-requirement passages were inspected on 30 October 2026. This is a historical engineering guide with collaborative contributions, not a current universal specification. Free institutional access was verified; blanket adaptation rights were not established. The reading is external, with credits retained.

Additional passages on programming, process loads, contamination requirements, thermal/utility planning, climate and layout were inspected for the facility formation. Assigned whole-guide reading remains broader than those inspected passages. Its historical standard references, airflow suggestions and sizing examples require their actual scope and edition; they are not transferred into universal design requirements.

